title: CSPM vs CNAPP and the rest
page: cspm-vs-cnapp.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: If you only buy one tool, buy a CSPM or a CNAPP's CSPM module; the other categories are layers you add as your environment matures.

== The 30-second version
layout: text
section: top
CSPM finds misconfigurations. CWPP protects running workloads. CIEM analyzes who can do what. DSPM finds and watches sensitive data. CNAPP bundles them. SSPM does CSPM's job for SaaS apps.
notes:
CSPM finds misconfigurations. CWPP protects what is running on your VMs and containers. CIEM analyzes who can do what across your IAM. DSPM finds and watches your sensitive data.

CNAPP, the Cloud-Native Application Protection Platform, is the umbrella that bundles all of those into one platform. SSPM does the same job as CSPM, but for SaaS apps such as Microsoft 365 and Salesforce instead of cloud accounts.

== If you only buy one tool
layout: text
section: top
Buy a CSPM, or the CSPM module of a CNAPP. It addresses 60-70% of cloud-security findings.
The other categories are layers you add as your environment matures.
notes:
The page's advice is blunt: if you only buy one tool, buy a CSPM, or the CSPM module of a CNAPP. It addresses 60-70% of cloud-security findings.

The other categories are layers you add as your environment matures. The rest of the deck is about when each layer earns its place.

== Six categories, six slices of risk
layout: table
section: tool-cards
row: Category | Slice of cloud risk
row: CSPM | Posture and misconfigurations across cloud accounts
row: CWPP | Workload protection: VMs, containers, runtime, vuln scan
row: CIEM | Identity entitlements: who can do what
row: DSPM | Find and classify sensitive data
row: SSPM | SaaS posture: M365, Salesforce, Workday config drift
row: CNAPP | Umbrella bundling CSPM, CWPP, CIEM and more
notes:
These are the six categories you will hear in vendor pitches, and each solves a different slice of cloud risk.

CSPM covers posture and misconfigurations. CWPP covers workloads. CIEM covers identity entitlements, including unused privileges. DSPM finds and classifies sensitive data. SSPM covers SaaS configuration drift. CNAPP is the umbrella that bundles CSPM, CWPP, CIEM and more in one platform.

== What each one watches
layout: table
section: comparison
row: Category | Watches | Primary signal
row: CSPM | Cloud account configuration | Cloud APIs, IaC scans
row: CWPP | Running VMs, containers, serverless | Agents, eBPF, syscalls
row: CIEM | IAM permissions across accounts | CloudTrail, IAM policies
row: DSPM | Data stores | Content scanning, classification
row: SSPM | SaaS apps | SaaS APIs, OAuth tokens
row: CNAPP | All of the above | Multi-source unified
notes:
The categories are easier to keep straight side by side. Each watches a different thing and reads a different signal.

CSPM reads cloud APIs and IaC scans. CWPP uses agents, eBPF and syscalls. CIEM reads CloudTrail and IAM policies. DSPM scans content and classifies it. SSPM reads SaaS APIs and OAuth tokens. CNAPP unifies several sources.

== When you need each one
layout: table
section: comparison
row: Category | Catches things like | When you need it
row: CSPM | Public S3, open SGs, missing MFA | From day one
row: CWPP | Vulnerable images, unauthorized processes | When you run K8s/VMs at scale
row: CIEM | Over-privileged roles, privilege paths | When IAM > ~50 roles
row: DSPM | PII in dev, shadow data | When you have regulated data
row: SSPM | Risky third-party app grants | When SaaS sprawl matters
row: CNAPP | Toxic combinations across categories | When tool sprawl hurts
notes:
The same comparison, read for timing. CSPM is needed from day one. CWPP matters when you run Kubernetes or VMs at scale. CIEM starts to matter when IAM grows past roughly 50 roles.

DSPM is for when you have regulated data. SSPM is for when SaaS sprawl matters. CNAPP is for when tool sprawl hurts.

== CSPM: the oldest category
layout: checklist
section: cspm
check: Continuous scanning across every cloud account
check: Findings mapped to CIS, AWS FSBP, NIST, PCI DSS, HIPAA
check: IaC scanning at PR time
check: Auto-remediation playbooks for common findings
check: Compliance reporting without manual evidence
notes:
CSPM is the oldest and most mature category. It scans your cloud accounts through their APIs and flags deviations from a security baseline: public S3 buckets, security groups open to the internet on port 22, unencrypted databases, IAM users without MFA, root-account API keys and missing CloudTrail.

Good CSPM scans continuously across every account, maps findings to standards, scans IaC such as Terraform, CloudFormation and Bicep at PR time so misconfigurations do not reach prod, offers auto-remediation playbooks, and produces compliance reporting that satisfies auditors without manual evidence collection.

== CSPM: strengths and the blind spot
layout: compare
section: cspm
col: Pros | Cheapest credible investment per dollar | Closes the bulk of "how did this happen?" findings | Every CNAPP vendor has a CSPM module
col: Cons | Sees nothing inside your workloads | Cannot tell if a process misbehaves | "0 findings" can give a false sense of security
notes:
The page calls CSPM the cheapest credible cloud-security investment per dollar. It closes the bulk of the "how did this happen?" findings before they happen, and it is a mature category.

The con is that it does not see inside your workloads. It cannot tell you whether a process is doing something it should not, only whether the surrounding configuration allows it. CSPM-alone teams sometimes get a false sense of security, "we have 0 findings", while runtime threats go undetected.

== CWPP: protecting what runs
layout: checklist
section: cwpp
check: Scan container images and VM disk images
check: Detect runtime threats and unauthorized processes
check: Baseline behavior, alert on change
check: Compliance evidence such as FIM for PCI
check: eBPF agents without kernel modules
notes:
CWPP protects the running workload: virtual machines, containers and serverless functions. It is the cloud-era successor to endpoint protection. Where EDR watches a Windows desktop, CWPP watches a Linux VM's syscalls or a Kubernetes pod's network and filesystem activity.

Good CWPP scans container and VM images, detects runtime threats, baselines behavior and alerts when a workload does something new, provides compliance evidence, and uses eBPF-based agents that need no kernel modules and have low overhead.

== CWPP: what it catches and what it costs
layout: compare
section: cwpp
col: Pros | Catches post-exploitation and persistence | Falco flags an interactive shell in a container
col: Cons | Agent management overhead | False-positive tuning is ongoing | Gaps on serverless
notes:
CWPP catches what CSPM cannot see: the post-exploitation and persistence stages of an attack. Falco's default rules, for example, flag an interactive shell opened inside a running container, the kind of hands-on activity that follows a compromise.

The costs are agent management overhead and false-positive tuning, which the page calls a real ongoing job. There are coverage gaps on serverless workloads, where you cannot install a host agent. Those rely on provider-side telemetry, or on an agent embedded in the function or image where the vendor supports one.

== CIEM: IAM as a graph problem
layout: checklist
section: ciem
check: Map effective permissions per identity
check: Find unused permissions and trim policies
check: Detect privilege-escalation chains
check: Cross-cloud visibility of federations and trust
check: Alert when a role drifts past its baseline
notes:
CIEM treats IAM as a graph. It analyzes every identity, whether user, role, service principal or workload identity, and every permission across every cloud account, finds privilege-escalation paths and surfaces over-permissioned roles.

Good CIEM maps effective permissions across policies, group memberships, SCPs and conditions. It finds unused permissions, such as a role with S3:* that only uses S3:GetObject. It detects chains such as iam:CreatePolicyVersion leading to admin, gives cross-cloud visibility, and flags drift.

== CIEM: fit and limits
layout: compare
section: ciem
col: Pros | Catches the Capital One pattern: over-privileged role plus SSRF | Mature tools make least privilege operational
col: Cons | Hard to operationalize at small scale | Deciding what counts as unused needs baselining
notes:
The page says CIEM is the thing that catches the Capital One pattern, an over-privileged role combined with SSRF. Mature tools turn IAM least privilege from a wish into an operational practice.

The cons: it is hard to operationalize at small scale, and if you have 5 IAM roles you do not need a graph database. Implementation effort is real, because tuning what counts as unused requires baselining and data review.

== DSPM: knowing where the data is
layout: checklist
section: dspm
check: Discover and classify PII, PCI, PHI, IP, secrets
check: Map data flow across accounts
check: Surface shadow data nobody tracks
check: Flag sensitive data open to broad principals
notes:
DSPM scans data stores such as S3, EBS snapshots, RDS, BigQuery and Snowflake, and tells you what sensitive data lives where, who can access it, and whether the surrounding controls match the data's classification. It is the newer category, emerging around 2022.

Good DSPM classifies data at scale without sampling, maps data flow, surfaces shadow data such as copies of regulated data in test environments or backup buckets, and ties classification to access policy.

== DSPM: value and caveats
layout: compare
section: dspm
col: Pros | A differentiator under regulatory burden | Catches what CSPM and CWPP miss
col: Cons | Terminology still shifting | Scanning costs can be material | Some regulated environments cannot ship data to a SaaS DSPM
notes:
DSPM is a differentiator for organizations with regulatory burden, such as healthcare, financial services and EU residents under GDPR. It catches things CSPM and CWPP miss because they are not looking at the actual data.

It is the newest category, so terminology and capabilities are still shifting. Scanning costs, especially at S3 scale, can be material. In some regulated environments you cannot ship customer data out of the account to a SaaS DSPM.

== SSPM: CSPM for SaaS
layout: text
section: sspm
CSPM's job for SaaS: Microsoft 365, Google Workspace, Salesforce, GitHub, Slack, Workday and others.
- Catalog OAuth apps with risk scores
- Find forgotten admin accounts
- Audit configurations against vendor benchmarks
- But coverage varies wildly by app; niche SaaS may be unsupported
notes:
SSPM does for SaaS what CSPM does for IaaS. It catches risky configurations, third-party OAuth grants and dormant admin accounts that traditional cloud security tools do not see.

The page says the OAuth-app problem alone is worth the cost for organizations with 100+ SaaS subscriptions. The con is that coverage varies wildly by app, since vendors integrate with the most popular apps first, and the long tail of niche SaaS may not be supported.

== Cost versus coverage
layout: figure
section: sspm
figure: cc-title
Approximate positions of the major tool categories, by cost and by coverage.
notes:
The figure places the major cloud security tool categories at approximate positions on a cost versus coverage plane. The page's caption names two patterns: open source covers a slice cheaply, and full CNAPP covers everything but costs the most.

Most teams blend a CNAPP with the provider-native free tier and one specialist, usually DSPM or CIEM, for what the CNAPP does not do well.

== AI-SPM and CDR
layout: figure
section: sspm | ai-spm-cdr
figure: newer-categories
AI-SPM inventories a cloud account's AI assets and the IAM that can reach them. CDR watches logs and telemetry to catch an attack in progress.
notes:
Vendors bolted on two categories after this taxonomy settled, and both are now folding into the CNAPP umbrella. AI-SPM inventories the AI assets in a cloud account, such as models, training-data stores, inference endpoints and agents, and the IAM that can reach them. It is CSPM's logic pointed at the AI stack.

CDR watches cloud audit logs, workload telemetry and identity events to catch an attack in progress, rather than scoring misconfigurations before one happens. Neither is usually bought standalone in 2026; they arrive as modules of the CNAPP or CWPP you already run.

== CNAPP: the umbrella
layout: figure
section: cnapp
figure: cn2-fig-title
Coined by Gartner in 2021. It bundles categories; it does not replace them.
notes:
CNAPP was coined by Gartner in 2021. It does not replace the other categories; it bundles them. A platform typically includes CSPM, CWPP, CIEM, IaC scanning, container vuln scanning and DSPM, with a unified data model and one console.

== The toxic combination
layout: text
section: cnapp
A CNAPP can say: this S3 bucket is public AND holds regulated PII AND is accessible by a workload identity reachable from the internet via a known-vulnerable container.
Cross-domain reasoning is the value.
notes:
A CSPM might tell you this S3 bucket is public. A separate CIEM might tell you this IAM role has unused permissions. A CNAPP can connect them: the bucket is public, contains regulated PII, and is accessible by a workload identity that is reachable from the internet via a known-vulnerable container.

The page says that cross-domain reasoning is the actual value, and that toxic-combination detection across modules is the unique CNAPP capability no single-category tool offers.

== CNAPP: pros and cons
layout: compare
section: cnapp
col: Pros | Operationally simpler from scratch | Cross-domain detections | One contract and training surface | Generally cheaper than separate tools at scale
col: Cons | Vendor lock-in | A mediocre module may win on integration; the reverse is true too | CNAPP is a market category, not a product spec
notes:
The pros are operational simplicity when starting from scratch, cross-domain detections that point tools cannot replicate, a single procurement, contract and training surface, and generally lower cost than buying the categories separately at scale.

The cons are vendor lock-in and variation. A CNAPP that is mediocre at one module may still beat your existing point tool because of integration leverage, but the reverse is true too. Best-of-breed arguments exist for a reason, and CNAPP is a market category, not a product spec: what one vendor calls CNAPP differs a lot in capability from another.

== Buying considerations
layout: checklist
section: cnapp
check: Coverage of YOUR clouds
check: Agent footprint versus agentless coverage
check: Integration with SIEM, ticketing and IaC
check: Detection quality, not rule count
check: Pricing model at your actual size
notes:
Check coverage of your own clouds, since some CNAPPs are strong on AWS but shallow on Azure and GCP. Agentless scanning is the baseline for coverage; an agent adds runtime detection and blocking at the cost of deploying and maintaining it.

Look at how well it integrates with your SIEM, ticketing and IaC pipelines. Anyone can ship 1,000 rules, so ask which fire and which are noise. Most vendors charge per workload or per billable resource, so do the math at your actual size.

== Which do I need: the early stages
layout: table
section: which-do-i-need
row: Your situation | Add
row: Workloads moved to the cloud last quarter | Provider-native CSPM; get findings to zero
row: A few hundred resources across AWS | Commercial CSPM, or a CNAPP's CSPM module
row: Kubernetes in production | CWPP
row: IAM past ~50 roles or 5 accounts | CIEM
notes:
The answer depends on your maturity stage. If you put workloads in the cloud last quarter, start with provider-native CSPM. It is cheap, decent and tied to the cloud you already use. Get the findings to zero before evaluating commercial tools.

With a few hundred resources across AWS, add a commercial CSPM, or the CSPM module of a CNAPP. The provider-native tools start to feel limited at this scale: too many findings, hard to triage, weak IaC scanning.

If you run Kubernetes in production, add CWPP. Falco is a free starting point if you have the operational chops. Definitely do Network Policies and Pod Security Standards too; those are configuration practices, not products. Once you cross roughly 50 IAM roles or 5 cloud accounts, CIEM starts paying off, and AWS IAM Access Analyzer is a free starting point.

== Which do I need: data, SaaS and tool sprawl
layout: table
section: which-do-i-need
row: Your situation | Add
row: Regulated data | DSPM
row: 200+ SaaS subscriptions | SSPM
row: 4 point tools, security ops drowning | CNAPP consolidation, after a serious POC
A POC is how you discover that consolidated does not always mean as good as the point tool you replace.
notes:
If you handle regulated data, add DSPM, especially if data flows through copy, snapshot or backup pipelines that nobody is tracking. AWS Macie is a starting point on AWS; commercial DSPM covers multi-cloud and finer classification.

With 200+ SaaS subscriptions, add SSPM. It is especially worth it if a distributed workforce makes OAuth-grant decisions you do not see.

With 4 different point tools and security ops drowning, CNAPP consolidation makes financial sense. Run a serious POC, because consolidated does not always mean as good as the point tool you are replacing.

== A credible program on open source
layout: table
section: open-source
row: Category | Open-source option
row: CSPM | Prowler, ScoutSuite, Steampipe
row: CWPP | Falco, Tetragon, Trivy
row: CIEM | Cloudsplaining, ROADtools
row: DSPM | Largely commercial; Presidio for PII
row: IaC | tfsec (now part of Trivy), Checkov, Conftest
Trade-off: more operational overhead than a managed CNAPP.
notes:
You can build a credible cloud-security program entirely on open source, especially if you are small or budget-constrained.

The page's combination is Prowler in CI for posture, Falco on K8s nodes for runtime, Cloudsplaining on a schedule for IAM drift, Macie or Presidio for data classification, and Trivy at PR time for image scanning. That is a credible program for free, with the trade-off of more operational overhead than a managed CNAPP.

== Is CNAPP just a marketing term?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider whether the bundled products are real capabilities or rebadged CSPM.
notes:
This is the first FAQ question. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== Real products, with marketing energy
layout: answer
section: faq
It started as a Gartner-coined category and draws marketing energy, but the bundled offerings are real capabilities, not rebadged CSPM. Cross-domain toxic-combination detections need a unified data model that point tools lack.
notes:
The page says there is marketing energy around CNAPP, but the bundled offerings are real product capabilities, not rebadged CSPM. The cross-domain toxic-combination detections are technically distinctive and require a unified data model that point tools do not have.

== Should I buy CNAPP or stay best-of-breed?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider scale, existing investments, team specialization and the vendors involved.
notes:
This is the second FAQ question. Ask it before showing the answer.

== Run the POC
layout: answer
section: faq
The answer depends on your situation. Most mid-size organizations end up at CNAPP; specialized shops often stay best-of-breed for their critical category and use a CNAPP for the rest.
notes:
The page says to run the POC, because the answer depends on your scale, existing investments, team specialization and the specific vendors.

Most mid-size organizations end up at CNAPP because operational simplification outweighs the per-module compromises. Specialized shops, such as heavy Kubernetes or heavy regulated data, often stay best-of-breed for their critical category and use a CNAPP for everything else.
